<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Epic of Sundiata</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">tells of the 13th century; handed down orally; first written summaries, in Arabic, before 1890</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as ߛߏ߲߬ߖߘߊ߬, Sònjàdà</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Epic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a> is an epic poem of the Malinke people about Sundiata Keita, the founder of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali-empire">Mali Empire</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-africa">West Africa</a>, who ruled from 1235 until his death in 1255. It has been told for generations by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/griots">griots</a>, or jelis, the traditional oral historians of the Mande world, and it is foundational to Mande culture.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>There is no single authoritative version, and the known variants together hold more than 40 episodes, but the core stays much the same. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghan-kon-fatta">Naré Maghann Konaté</a>, told by a soothsaying hunter that an ugly wife would bear him a mighty king, marries the hunchbacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogolon-conde">Sogolon</a>, though he already has a son, Dankaran Toumani, by Sassouma Bereté. Sogolon's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a> cannot walk as a child and is mocked by Sassouma. After the king's death Dankaran Toumani takes the throne; insulted, Sundiata breaks an iron rod trying to raise himself and finally stands with the help of a baobab branch.</p><p>Sassouma's hatred drives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a>, his mother and sisters into exile in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mema">Mema</a> kingdom, where he grows into a warrior and is made heir to its throne. When Soumaoro Kanté, a historical leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sosso-empire">Sosso</a> portrayed in the epic as a cruel sorcerer king, attacks the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mandinka">Mandinka</a> and Dankaran Toumani flees, the people send for Sundiata. He forms a coalition of small kingdoms, defeats the Sosso, is crowned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mansa">Mansa</a> and gives the nobles an oral constitution, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kouroukan-fouga">Kouroukan Fouga</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Discovery and study</h2><p>Arabic sources of the 14th century, notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-khaldun">Ibn Khaldun</a>, say little of early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, so the oral tradition may be critical for reconstructing its history, though it has changed over the centuries. Written summaries in Arabic existed before 1890; French officials collected versions in the 1890s, and translations into French and German appeared from 1898. Western-educated West Africans produced literary versions from the 1930s, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecole-william-ponty">École William Ponty</a> staged a drama of the story in 1937. Djibril Tamsir Niane's French version of 1960, from the telling of the jeli Djeli Mamoudou Kouyate, became the modern standard, and the first line-by-line transcription of a jeli's performance was made in 1967.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The epic also conveys Mande ideas of family. Fadenya, the rivalry between half-siblings, appears in the enmity between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sundiata-keita">Sundiata</a> and Dankaran Toumani; badenya, the affection between children of one mother, in the support of his sister Kolonkan and his siblings. Sundiata needs both to fulfil his destiny. Jelis still retell the epic, and it is performed in masked rituals. It is part of the official national mythology of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-gambia">Gambia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/senegal">Senegal</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a> and is studied in their primary schools.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Epic of Sundiata” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
